messages:
maria: OK team — planning a synthetic surge probe tomorrow 09:00 UTC to validate backpressure and admission controls across dedicated pools. Goal: find the queue-length threshold where we start rejecting vs queuing and verify graceful fallback to cheaper model variants.

sam: +1. high-level approach? small ramp then 5m plateau, repeated bursts? also which regions?

maria: plan: two-region parallel test (us-west1 + eu-central1). ramp 0->400 rps over 3m, plateau 10m, then 3x 30s spike bursts. repeat with longer sequences if stable.

raj: we should vary payload token lengths. realistic mix: 60% short (<=32 tokens), 30% medium (100-256), 10% long (512+). KV cache warm-up matters for cost.

ingrid: admission settings to exercise: admission.max_in_flight per pool, admission.queue_limit, and route-level rate_limit. Proposed toggles: {"max_in_flight": 250, "queue_limit": 2000, "route_qps": 600} for dedicated-gpu-pool-a.

dave: cost question — do we run on reserved Dedicated test pool or spin ephemeral on hosted? Dedicated will show real saturation patterns but costs more. We have credits on dedicated-test-1.

maria: use dedicated-test-1 for primary, fallback to hosted-ver-quant for cheap variant when admission rejects. We'll measure failover times.

sam: metrics to capture: GPU utilization (per-GPU%), GPU memory alloc, scheduler queue length, admission rejections, rejected_by_policy, request_latency p50/p95/p99, token_rate, billing tokens. Also ingress egress network drops.

raj: add a custom tag to synthetic requests: X-Loadtest: surge-probe-1768805123 so logs/trace filtering is easy.

maria: load generator: k6 in distributed mode. sample snippet: ```js
import http from 'k6/http';
import { sleep } from 'k6';
export default function() {
  http.post('https://api.redwood.example/v1/generate', JSON.stringify({prompt: 'summarize ...', max_tokens: 300}), {
    headers: { 'Authorization': 'Bearer $LOADTEST_KEY', 'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'X-Loadtest': 'surge-probe-1768805123' }
  });
  sleep(0.1);
}
```

ingrid: add a separate job that warms KV cache: 5k short-prefetch requests 30m before test against same model/version.

maria: abort conditions (manual+automated) — auto-abort if: 1) admission.rejections > 5% sustained for 2m and queue_length > 3000; 2) p99 request_latency > 6s for 3m; 3) any GPU OOMs or node reboots. Manual abort if customer-impacting alerts fire.

dave: loadtest-bot can trigger abort if the prometheus rule fires? @loadtest-bot create alert rule linking to pager if auto-abort executed.

loadtest-bot: noted. I will publish an alert rule: name=surge-probe-autopause-1768805123, expr=(sum(admission_rejections_total{test="surge-probe-1768805123"}) / sum(requests_total{test="surge-probe-1768805123"})) > 0.05 for 2m OR histogram_quantile(0.99, rate(request_duration_seconds_bucket{test="surge-probe-1768805123"}[3m])) > 6

raj: we'll also log model-switch latency: time between original route fail and fallback model responding. target < 800ms.

sam: one nuance: network egress quotas in eu-central1 are lower. reduce worker counts there to avoid hitting infra caps and skewing results. maybe 60% traffic from us-west1, 40% eu.

maria: schedule: warmup 08:20-08:50 UTC; functional smoke 08:55; main run 09:00-09:25; extended analysis window 09:30-10:00. Postmortem doc link will be created.

dave: I'll provision dedicated-test-1 (8xA100) and pre-create autoscaler policy snapshots to revert. also will take a snapshot of the model serving config.

ingrid: runbook steps for operator: 1) how to pause test (curl to k6 controller), 2) how to flip admission to strict reject only mode: `rdctl admission set --pool dedicated-test-1 --mode reject` 3) how to re-open. include exact commands.

maria: please add minimal runbook in thread before EOD. tagging @sam for telemetry dashboards and @dave for infra prep. thanks all. :rocket:
